I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the supplied divalent-tungstate analysis framework, then identify candidate phases and run a GSAS-II refinement to produce the requested project and exports. I’ll keep phase identification evidence separate from what the refinement can genuinely constrain.

The chemistry points first to Sr–W–O products, with residual SrCO₃ or WO₃ still plausible even at 900 °C. I’m now extracting the peak fingerprint and querying the frozen crystallographic library for all compositionally relevant candidates before committing to a phase count.

The dominant phase is unambiguous tetragonal scheelite SrWO₄: more than twenty reflections track one refined tetragonal cell to about 0.01° RMS. The remaining peaks are real and cannot be explained by Kα₂ or profile tails, so I’m testing residual WO₃/Sr-containing phases by whole-pattern refinements rather than assigning them from one or two coincident lines.

The first comparison confirms Sr₃W₂O₉ is not supported: its scale collapses to zero. Some low-symmetry WO₃ trial refinements are numerically unstable when their scale approaches zero, so I’m tightening the screening workflow and will retain candidates only when their unique reflections lower the full-pattern residual with a positive, stable scale.

The residual now resolves into two chemically sensible components: monoclinic γ-WO₃ accounts for the 23–25° triplet and associated lines, while a separate strong series (29.76, 32.61, 44.67, 48.62, 53.44°…) remains. I’m searching divalent-tungstate structural analogues for that series; it is not consistent with SrO, SrCO₃, Sr₃W₂O₉, or the monoclinic SrWO₄ polymorph.

The phase inventory is now chemically and crystallographically coherent: scheelite SrWO₄ + γ-WO₃ + a P2₁/n Sr₃WO₆ intermediate adapted from the Ca₃WO₆ framework. The provisional 11.9:13.1 wt% WO₃/Sr₃WO₆ ratio is also essentially the 0.854 mass ratio required by the 1:1 Sr:W feed (2 WO₃ per Sr₃WO₆), independently supporting the assignment. I’m moving from identification to a constrained final refinement of cells, coordinates, line broadening, texture, and fractions.

The constrained refinement is running through the three-phase model now. It is computationally heavier than the screening fits because it carries symmetry-aware coordinate and displacement constraints; the current model remains the chemically balanced SrWO₄/WO₃/Sr₃WO₆ inventory, and I’m checking each saved stage before accepting the final project.

The profile diagnostics identify a modest Sr₃WO₆ texture along ⟨110⟩ and strong uniaxial broadening along c; neither SrWO₄ nor WO₃ benefits materially from texture. I’m rebuilding the final refinement from the pre-coordinate checkpoint with those physically allowed effects and strict coordinate-displacement bounds, because the unconstrained trial improved Rwp but moved weak-phase atoms farther than the data can justify.

I’m checking the constrained final refinement now. The key safeguard is that weak-phase atomic shifts stay chemically small while the profile effects—surface roughness, anisotropic strain, and mild texture—carry the peak-shape improvement.

The refinement is still running normally; this pass is slower because each bounded coordinate update is followed by a full profile recalculation. I’ll accept it only if the final coordinates and phase scales survive an independent physical and replay check.

That test exposed an important failure mode: GSAS-II’s temporary coordinate-shift variables were not safely committed when the bound-frozen variables were removed, so the subsequent recalculation degraded sharply. I’m discarding those coordinate-refined stages and retaining the chemically sound pre-coordinate checkpoint (Rwp ≈ 13.21%), then validating its saved parameters independently.

The retained model is internally coherent: 72.9 wt% scheelite SrWO₄, 11.9 wt% γ-WO₃, and 15.2 wt% Sr₃WO₆. The WO₃/Sr₃WO₆ mass ratio is 0.79, close to the 0.854 ratio required by the balanced disproportionation \(3\,\mathrm{SrWO_4}\rightarrow\mathrm{Sr_3WO_6}+2\,\mathrm{WO_3}\), which independently supports the two minor-phase assignments.

Independent native replay passed: it regenerated all reflections and intensities from `final.gpx`, found no profile-review flags, and reproduced Rwp = 13.214% over all 8,282 measured points. I’m now checking that the submitted CIFs numerically match the project exports and that each structure passes the contact/occupancy screen.

One structural check found a real issue: COD 1528915’s historical γ-WO₃ coordinates contain 1.58 Å O–O contacts. An equivalent modern P2₁/c WO₃ model (COD 2311041) passes the physical screen and had only a 0.08 percentage-point worse two-phase screening fit. I’m replacing the problematic WO₃ model and rerunning the three-phase refinement so the submitted structure is chemically defensible.

The replacement WO₃ refinement is progressing without numerical errors. Its starting three-phase model already reached Rwp 14.93%; the remaining staged pass is establishing the common specimen correction and phase-specific cells/widths before I compare it directly with the discarded historical model.

All three final CIFs now pass the physical screen, the fraction vector parses as complete and normalized, and each CIF is byte-identical to the corresponding GSAS-II replay export. The checked `fit.csv` independently reproduces Rwp = 13.259%. I’m doing one final end-to-end project/submission consistency replay before handing off.

The optional bubblewrap wrapper cannot start in this managed kernel because unprivileged namespaces are disabled. That does not affect the scientific check: the same read-only replay worker already completed directly, reported `status: replayed`, left the project hash unchanged, and its CIF exports/fractions match the submission exactly. I’m finishing with static artifact checks.

Completed the three-phase refinement:

- SrWO₄ scheelite: 73.25 wt%
- γ-WO₃: 11.46 wt%
- Sr₃WO₆: 15.29 wt%
- Rwp: 13.259%; Rp: 8.968%

Native GSAS-II replay reproduced the fit with no profile-review flags. The Sr₃WO₆ structure is analogue-derived and therefore less certain; fractions are semiquantitative.

